pg| ***** 30 ***** |


pc|C4|pc|C2|pc|CK|pc|C3|
pg| ***** 31 ***** |


pc|CQ|pc|C5|pc|C6|pc|CT|
pg| ***** 32 ***** |


pc|S9|pc|SA|pc|S8|pc|S2|
pg| ***** 33 ***** |


pc|D8|pc|D6|pc|DK|pc|DA|
pg| ***** 34 ***** |


mc|12|
pg| ***** 35 ***** |


UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Nicely done North. |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=kickback,Kickback^*N|CP||FP||at| RKCB can be very useful because it allows you to stop at 5 of a minor while still bidding RKCB. Whereas if you use 4N as RKCB you can get too high when a minor suit (especially clubs) is trump.|
PG| ***** 36 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The issue is you have to understand when 4@D is RKCB and when it is natural. Many partnerships have elaborate rules about that. But my rule is, if a bid can be |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=kickback,Kickback^*N|CP||FP||at| RKCB, assume it is.|
PG| ***** 37 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|So on this auction, since I raised clubs, any time partner bids 4@D, that should be RKCB.|
PG| ***** 38 ***** |


